BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* (PEP: 8) Tj T* (Title: Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* (Author: Guido van Rossum <guido@python.org>,) Tj T* (Barry Warsaw <barry@python.org>,) Tj T* (Alyssa Coghlan <ncoghlan@gmail.com>) Tj T* (Status: Active) Tj T* (Type: Process) Tj T* (Created: 05-Jul-2001) Tj T* (Post-History: 05-Jul-2001, 01-Aug-2013) Tj T* () Tj T* (Introduction) Tj T* () Tj T* (This document gives coding conventions for the Python code comprising the standard library in) Tj T* (the main Python distribution.  Please see the companion informational PEP describing style) Tj T* (guidelines for the C code in the C implementation of Python.) Tj T* () Tj T* (This document and PEP 257 \(Docstring Conventions\) were adapted from Guido's original Python) Tj T* (Style Guide essay, with some additions from Barry's style guide [2].) Tj T* () Tj T* (This style guide evolves over time as additional conventions are identified and past conventions) Tj T* (are rendered obsolete by changes in the language itself.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Many projects have their own coding style guidelines. In the event of any conflicts, such) Tj T* (project-specific guides take precedence for that project.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A Foolish Consistency is the Hobgoblin of Little Minds) Tj T* () Tj T* (One of Guido's key insights is that code is read much more often than it is written.  The) Tj T* (guidelines provided here are intended to improve the readability of code and make it consistent) Tj T* (across the wide spectrum of Python code.  As PEP 20 says, "Readability counts".) Tj T* () Tj T* (A style guide is about consistency.  Consistency with this style guide is important.) Tj T* (Consistency within a project is more important. Consistency within one module or function is the) Tj T* (most important.) Tj T* () Tj T* (However, know when to be inconsistent -- sometimes style guide recommendations just aren't) Tj T* (applicable.  When in doubt, use your best judgment.  Look at other examples and decide what) Tj T* (looks best.  And don't hesitate to ask!) Tj T* () Tj T* (In particular: do not break backwards compatibility just to comply with this PEP!) Tj T* () Tj T* (Some other good reasons to ignore a particular guideline:) Tj T* () Tj T* (1. When applying the guideline would make the code less readable, even for someone who is used) Tj T* (to reading code that follows this PEP.) Tj T* () Tj T* (2. To be consistent with surrounding code that also breaks it \(maybe for historic reasons\) --) Tj T* (although this is also an opportunity to clean up someone else's mess \(in true XP style\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (3. Because the code in question predates the introduction of the guideline and there is no other) Tj T* (reason to be modifying that code.) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 1 of 27) Tj T* ET
endstream
