BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Tabs or Spaces?) Tj T* () Tj T* (Spaces are the preferred indentation method.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Tabs should be used solely to remain consistent with code that is already indented with tabs.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Python disallows mixing tabs and spaces for indentation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Maximum Line Length) Tj T* () Tj T* (Limit all lines to a maximum of 79 characters.) Tj T* () Tj T* (For flowing long blocks of text with fewer structural restrictions \(docstrings or comments\), the) Tj T* (line length should be limited to 72 characters.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Limiting the required editor window width makes it possible to have several files open side by) Tj T* (side, and works well when using code review tools that present the two versions in adjacent) Tj T* (columns.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The default wrapping in most tools disrupts the visual structure of the code, making it more) Tj T* (difficult to understand. The limits are chosen to avoid wrapping in editors with the window) Tj T* (width set to 80, even if the tool places a marker glyph in the final column when wrapping lines.) Tj T* (Some web based tools may not offer dynamic line wrapping at all.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Some teams strongly prefer a longer line length.  For code maintained exclusively or primarily) Tj T* (by a team that can reach agreement on this issue, it is okay to increase the line length limit) Tj T* (up to 99 characters, provided that comments and docstrings are still wrapped at 72 characters.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Python standard library is conservative and requires limiting lines to 79 characters \(and) Tj T* (docstrings/comments to 72\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (The preferred way of wrapping long lines is by using Python's implied line continuation inside) Tj T* (parentheses, brackets and braces.  Long lines can be broken over multiple lines by wrapping) Tj T* (expressions in parentheses. These should be used in preference to using a backslash for line) Tj T* (continuation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Backslashes may still be appropriate at times.  For example, long, multiple with-statements) Tj T* (could not use implicit continuation before Python 3.10, so backslashes were acceptable for that) Tj T* (case:) Tj T* () Tj T* (with open\('/path/to/some/file/you/want/to/read'\) as file_1, \\) Tj T* (open\('/path/to/some/file/being/written', 'w'\) as file_2:) Tj T* (file_2.write\(file_1.read\(\)\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (\(See the previous discussion on `multiline if-statements`_ for further thoughts on the) Tj T* (indentation of such multiline with-statements.\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Another such case is with assert statements.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Make sure to indent the continued line appropriately.) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 4 of 27) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
