BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* (Should a Line Break Before or After a Binary Operator?) Tj T* () Tj T* (For decades the recommended style was to break after binary operators. But this can hurt) Tj T* (readability in two ways: the operators tend to get scattered across different columns on the) Tj T* (screen, and each operator is moved away from its operand and onto the previous line.  Here, the) Tj T* (eye has to do extra work to tell which items are added and which are subtracted:) Tj T* () Tj T* (# Wrong:) Tj T* (# operators sit far away from their operands) Tj T* (income = \(gross_wages +) Tj T* (taxable_interest +) Tj T* (\(dividends - qualified_dividends\) -) Tj T* (ira_deduction -) Tj T* (student_loan_interest\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (To solve this readability problem, mathematicians and their publishers follow the opposite) Tj T* (convention.  Donald Knuth explains the traditional rule in his *Computers and Typesetting*) Tj T* (series: "Although formulas within a paragraph always break after binary operations and) Tj T* (relations, displayed formulas always break before binary operations" [3].) Tj T* () Tj T* (Following the tradition from mathematics usually results in more readable code:) Tj T* () Tj T* (# Correct:) Tj T* (# easy to match operators with operands) Tj T* (income = \(gross_wages) Tj T* (+ taxable_interest) Tj T* (+ \(dividends - qualified_dividends\)) Tj T* (- ira_deduction) Tj T* (- student_loan_interest\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (In Python code, it is permissible to break before or after a binary operator, as long as the) Tj T* (convention is consistent locally.  For new code Knuth's style is suggested.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Blank Lines) Tj T* () Tj T* (Surround top-level function and class definitions with two blank lines.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Method definitions inside a class are surrounded by a single blank line.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Extra blank lines may be used \(sparingly\) to separate groups of related functions.  Blank lines) Tj T* (may be omitted between a bunch of related one-liners \(e.g. a set of dummy implementations\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (Use blank lines in functions, sparingly, to indicate logical sections.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Python accepts the control-L \(i.e. ^L\) form feed character as whitespace; many tools treat these) Tj T* (characters as page separators, so you may use them to separate pages of related sections of your) Tj T* (file. Note, some editors and web-based code viewers may not recognize control-L as a form feed) Tj T* (and will show another glyph in its place.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Source File Encoding) Tj T* () Tj T* (Code in the core Python distribution should always use UTF-8, and should not have an encoding) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 5 of 27) Tj T* ET
endstream
