BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* (completely consistent -- nevertheless, here are the currently recommended naming standards.  New) Tj T* (modules and packages \(including third party frameworks\) should be written to these standards,) Tj T* (but where an existing library has a different style, internal consistency is preferred.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Overriding Principle) Tj T* () Tj T* (Names that are visible to the user as public parts of the API should follow conventions that) Tj T* (reflect usage rather than implementation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Descriptive: Naming Styles) Tj T* () Tj T* (There are a lot of different naming styles.  It helps to be able to recognize what naming style) Tj T* (is being used, independently from what they are used for.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The following naming styles are commonly distinguished:) Tj T* () Tj T* (- b \(single lowercase letter\)) Tj T* (- B \(single uppercase letter\)) Tj T* (- lowercase) Tj T* (- lower_case_with_underscores) Tj T* (- UPPERCASE) Tj T* (- UPPER_CASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES) Tj T* (- CapitalizedWords \(or CapWords, or CamelCase -- so named because) Tj T* (of the bumpy look of its letters [4]\).  This is also sometimes known) Tj T* (as StudlyCaps.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note: When using acronyms in CapWords, capitalize all the) Tj T* (letters of the acronym.  Thus HTTPServerError is better than) Tj T* (HttpServerError.) Tj T* (- mixedCase \(differs from CapitalizedWords by initial lowercase) Tj T* (character!\)) Tj T* (- Capitalized_Words_With_Underscores \(ugly!\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (There's also the style of using a short unique prefix to group related names together.  This is) Tj T* (not used much in Python, but it is mentioned for completeness.  For example, the os.stat\(\)) Tj T* (function returns a tuple whose items traditionally have names like st_mode, st_size, st_mtime) Tj T* (and so on.  \(This is done to emphasize the correspondence with the fields of the POSIX system) Tj T* (call struct, which helps programmers familiar with that.\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (The X11 library uses a leading X for all its public functions.  In Python, this style is) Tj T* (generally deemed unnecessary because attribute and method names are prefixed with an object, and) Tj T* (function names are prefixed with a module name.) Tj T* () Tj T* (In addition, the following special forms using leading or trailing underscores are recognized) Tj T* (\(these can generally be combined with any case convention\):) Tj T* () Tj T* (- _single_leading_underscore: weak "internal use" indicator.) Tj T* (E.g. from M import * does not import objects whose names start) Tj T* (with an underscore.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- single_trailing_underscore_: used by convention to avoid) Tj T* (conflicts with Python keyword, e.g. :) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 15 of 27) Tj T* ET
