BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* (Python mangles these names with the class name: if class Foo has an attribute named __a, it) Tj T* (cannot be accessed by Foo.__a.  \(An insistent user could still gain access by calling) Tj T* (Foo._Foo__a.\) Generally, double leading underscores should be used only to avoid name conflicts) Tj T* (with attributes in classes designed to be subclassed.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note: there is some controversy about the use of __names \(see below\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (Constants) Tj T* () Tj T* (Constants are usually defined on a module level and written in all capital letters with) Tj T* (underscores separating words.  Examples include MAX_OVERFLOW and TOTAL.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Designing for Inheritance) Tj T* () Tj T* (Always decide whether a class's methods and instance variables \(collectively: "attributes"\)) Tj T* (should be public or non-public.  If in doubt, choose non-public; it's easier to make it public) Tj T* (later than to make a public attribute non-public.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Public attributes are those that you expect unrelated clients of your class to use, with your) Tj T* (commitment to avoid backwards incompatible changes.  Non-public attributes are those that are) Tj T* (not intended to be used by third parties; you make no guarantees that non-public attributes) Tj T* (won't change or even be removed.) Tj T* () Tj T* (We don't use the term "private" here, since no attribute is really private in Python \(without a) Tj T* (generally unnecessary amount of work\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (Another category of attributes are those that are part of the "subclass API" \(often called) Tj T* ("protected" in other languages\).  Some classes are designed to be inherited from, either to) Tj T* (extend or modify aspects of the class's behavior.  When designing such a class, take care to) Tj T* (make explicit decisions about which attributes are public, which are part of the subclass API,) Tj T* (and which are truly only to be used by your base class.) Tj T* () Tj T* (With this in mind, here are the Pythonic guidelines:) Tj T* () Tj T* (- Public attributes should have no leading underscores.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- If your public attribute name collides with a reserved keyword,) Tj T* (append a single trailing underscore to your attribute name.  This is) Tj T* (preferable to an abbreviation or corrupted spelling.  \(However,) Tj T* (notwithstanding this rule, 'cls' is the preferred spelling for any) Tj T* (variable or argument which is known to be a class, especially the) Tj T* (first argument to a class method.\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 1: See the argument name recommendation above for class methods.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- For simple public data attributes, it is best to expose just the) Tj T* (attribute name, without complicated accessor/mutator methods.  Keep) Tj T* (in mind that Python provides an easy path to future enhancement,) Tj T* (should you find that a simple data attribute needs to grow) Tj T* (functional behavior.  In that case, use properties to hide) Tj T* (functional implementation behind simple data attribute access) Tj T* (syntax.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 18 of 27) Tj T* ET
