BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 1: Try to keep the functional behavior side-effect free, although side-effects such as) Tj T* (caching are generally fine.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 2: Avoid using properties for computationally expensive operations; the attribute notation) Tj T* (makes the caller believe that access is \(relatively\) cheap.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- If your class is intended to be subclassed, and you have attributes) Tj T* (that you do not want subclasses to use, consider naming them with) Tj T* (double leading underscores and no trailing underscores.  This) Tj T* (invokes Python's name mangling algorithm, where the name of the) Tj T* (class is mangled into the attribute name.  This helps avoid) Tj T* (attribute name collisions should subclasses inadvertently contain) Tj T* (attributes with the same name.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 1: Note that only the simple class name is used in the mangled name, so if a subclass) Tj T* (chooses both the same class name and attribute name, you can still get name collisions.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 2: Name mangling can make certain uses, such as debugging and __getattr__\(\), less) Tj T* (convenient.  However the name mangling algorithm is well documented and easy to perform) Tj T* (manually.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Note 3: Not everyone likes name mangling.  Try to balance the need to avoid accidental name) Tj T* (clashes with potential use by advanced callers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Public and Internal Interfaces) Tj T* () Tj T* (Any backwards compatibility guarantees apply only to public interfaces. Accordingly, it is) Tj T* (important that users be able to clearly distinguish between public and internal interfaces.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Documented interfaces are considered public, unless the documentation explicitly declares them) Tj T* (to be provisional or internal interfaces exempt from the usual backwards compatibility) Tj T* (guarantees. All undocumented interfaces should be assumed to be internal.) Tj T* () Tj T* (To better support introspection, modules should explicitly declare the names in their public API) Tj T* (using the __all__ attribute. Setting __all__ to an empty list indicates that the module has no) Tj T* (public API.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Even with __all__ set appropriately, internal interfaces \(packages, modules, classes, functions,) Tj T* (attributes or other names\) should still be prefixed with a single leading underscore.) Tj T* () Tj T* (An interface is also considered internal if any containing namespace \(package, module or class\)) Tj T* (is considered internal.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Imported names should always be considered an implementation detail. Other modules must not rely) Tj T* (on indirect access to such imported names unless they are an explicitly documented part of the) Tj T* (containing module's API, such as os.path or a package's __init__ module that exposes) Tj T* (functionality from submodules.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Programming Recommendations) Tj T* () Tj T* (- Code should be written in a way that does not disadvantage other) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 19 of 27) Tj T* ET
endstream
