BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (PEP 8 Style Guide for Python Code) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (# Wrong:) Tj T* (if greeting is True:) Tj T* () Tj T* (- Use of the flow control statements return/break/continue) Tj T* (within the finally suite of a try...finally, where the flow control) Tj T* (statement would jump outside the finally suite, is discouraged.  This) Tj T* (is because such statements will implicitly cancel any active exception) Tj T* (that is propagating through the finally suite:) Tj T* () Tj T* (.. code-block:) Tj T* () Tj T* (# Wrong:) Tj T* (def foo\(\):) Tj T* (try:) Tj T* (1 / 0) Tj T* (finally:) Tj T* (return 42) Tj T* () Tj T* (Function Annotations) Tj T* () Tj T* (With the acceptance of PEP 484, the style rules for function annotations have changed.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- Function annotations should use PEP 484 syntax \(there are some) Tj T* (formatting recommendations for annotations in the previous section\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (- The experimentation with annotation styles that was recommended) Tj T* (previously in this PEP is no longer encouraged.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- However, outside the stdlib, experiments within the rules of PEP 484) Tj T* (are now encouraged.  For example, marking up a large third party) Tj T* (library or application with PEP 484 style type annotations,) Tj T* (reviewing how easy it was to add those annotations, and observing) Tj T* (whether their presence increases code understandability.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- The Python standard library should be conservative in adopting such) Tj T* (annotations, but their use is allowed for new code and for big) Tj T* (refactorings.) Tj T* () Tj T* (- For code that wants to make a different use of function annotations) Tj T* (it is recommended to put a comment of the form:) Tj T* () Tj T* (.. code-block:) Tj T* () Tj T* (# type: ignore) Tj T* () Tj T* (near the top of the file; this tells type checkers to ignore all annotations.  \(More fine-) Tj T* (grained ways of disabling complaints from type checkers can be found in PEP 484.\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (- Like linters, type checkers are optional, separate tools.  Python) Tj T* (interpreters by default should not issue any messages due to type) Tj T* (checking and should not alter their behavior based on annotations.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 25 of 27) Tj T* ET
